<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dawda Jawara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1924">1924</a>–<a href="/years/2019">2019</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dawda Jawara was a Gambian veterinarian and politician who led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">The Gambia</a> to independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1965. His People's Progressive Party won the elections of 1962, making him head of government, and he became president when the country became a republic in 1970. He governed until 1994, when he was overthrown in a military coup led by Yahya Jammeh.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dawda-jawara.jpg" alt="Dawda Jawara" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fernando Pereira / Anefo · CC BY-SA 3.0 nl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dawda_Jawara_(1979).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dawda Kairaba Jawara was born on 16 May 1924 at Barajally in the central river region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">The Gambia</a>, the son of Almammi Jawara, a well-to-do trader from an aristocratic family, and Mamma Fatty. As a child he attended local Arabic schools to memorise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>. He was later educated at the Methodist Boys' School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banjul">Banjul</a> and at Achimota College in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>, and trained as a veterinary surgeon at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>.</p><p>He returned in 1953 as a veterinary officer. Travelling the country to vaccinate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, he made contacts among cattle owners, district chiefs and village heads, who later formed the bulk of his early political support. Adults in the colony area around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banjul">Bathurst</a> had the vote, but those in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> did not. In 1959 the People's Progressive Society changed its name to challenge the urban-based parties, and Jawara became secretary of the party and was elected to the House of Representatives in 1960.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After the elections of 1962 Jawara became prime minister, self-government followed in 1963 and independence came on 18 February 1965, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a> remaining head of state. In 1970 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">The Gambia</a> became a republic and he was elected its first president. On 29 July 1981 an attempted coup led by Kukoi Samba Sanyang was defeated with the help of Senegalese troops, whom Jawara had asked for aid; several hundred people were killed. In December 1981 treaties in Dakar created the Senegambian Confederation, with Abdou Diouf as president and Jawara as vice-president; it collapsed in 1989.</p><p>In 1985 his government began the Economic Recovery Program, an adjustment programme drawn up with economists from the Harvard Institute for International Development and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">International Monetary Fund</a>. In December 1991 he announced that he would not seek re-election in 1992, but after the announcement was met with panic he agreed to stand again and won 56% of the vote.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 22 July 1994 soldiers led by Lieutenant Yahya Jammeh took the capital. Jawara was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> by an American warship and lived in exile until 2002, and the new ruling council dissolved the constitution. He returned as an elder statesman but was forbidden to take part in politics, and he died on 27 August 2019. His portrait appeared on Gambian dalasi banknotes and coins from 1971 to 1994. He was knighted in the 1966 New Year Honours and was the last living Gambian to have received a knighthood under the monarchy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dawda Jawara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Dawda Jawara</b>, who had held office since 1962, led the country, and it became a republic on 24 April 1970 after a second referendum.</blockquote><a href="/state/gambia">Gambia</a></li><li><blockquote>Constitutional reforms in the 1950s and early 1960s brought <b>Dawda Jawara</b> and his People's Progressive Party to power.</blockquote><a href="/state/gambia-colony-and-protectorate">Gambia Colony and Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Barajally, in what is now <a href="/countries/gambia">the Gambia</a>. Died in Fajara, in what is now <a href="/countries/gambia">the Gambia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.6,-14.95&amp;year=1985" aria-label="Barajally on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4140 1657 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Dawda Jawara"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4590 1927h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4590 1927h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=13.6,-14.95&amp;year=1985">What was here in 1985? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
